WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.270
Avengers: Endgame has made more money than
any movie ever, but it hasn't sold the most

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.300
tickets — in fact, it isn't even in the
top 15.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.660
From romantic epics to horror hits, here's
a look at all the blockbusters from years

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.920
past whose crowd sizes would have impressed
even Thanos.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.460
"Impossible."

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.360
The first film on our list is Ben-Hur.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.440
No, we aren't talking about the infamous 2016
bomb that lost Paramount a mind-numbing $120

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.100
million.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.020
Younger filmgoers may not have known it, but
that recent Ben-Hur was based on a 1959 multimillion-dollar

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.400
epic that was one of the biggest hits of all
time.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.180
The film starred Charlton Heston, one of Hollywood's
most popular and successful actors.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.220
Still, with a then-astronomical budget of
$15 million, Ben-Hur was a massive gamble

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.350
by MGM, which was hoping that the movie could
pull it out of impending bankruptcy.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.300
If Ben-Hur was their last hope, it answered
all their prayers.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:01:00.000
Fortunately for the studio, the epic made
$74 million domestically, which is $883 million

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.320
in today's money.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.540
All told, the blockbuster saved MGM from bankruptcy
and became acknowledged as a classic that's

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.900
still being talked about today.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.960
In 1977, the original Star Wars, also known
as A New Hope, more than conquered the box

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:14.580
office.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.980
It forever changed the movie landscape and
popular culture, in general.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.720
"You know, sometimes I amaze even myself."

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.140
Naturally, its first sequel was highly anticipated
— so much so that 1980's The Empire Strikes

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:32.080
Back opened to nearly $5 million in just 126
theaters during its initial limited run, and

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.220
$10 million once it expanded to 823 theaters.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.939
Even today, with our multiplex culture, those
numbers would be impressive, but when adjusted

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.200
for inflation, they would be earth-shaking.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.840
All told, Empire's initial run earned more
than $209 million, but it wasn't finished.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.440
The film was re-released several more times.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.500
In today's numbers, its total domestic gross
stands at $884 million, beating out Avengers:

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.160
Endgame by $30 million.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.520
"Guess what, Cap?

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:00.960
We lost."

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:07.920
Everyone loves dogs, Disney, and cartoons,
so it's no surprise that 1961's 101 Dalmatians,

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.380
which combines all three, was a huge moneymaker.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:15.220
The film is a classic from the tail end of
Disney's golden era, with a quirky story,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.440
some catchy tunes, and an iconic villain in
Cruella de Vil.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.480
It was a big hit in its initial run but the
film made most of its money in subsequent

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.480
re-releases.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:29.340
In fact, the 1991 re-release, celebrating
the movie's 30th anniversary, made $60 million.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.340
Clearly, the spotted pups had more staying
power than its initial release would have

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:33.840
suggested.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:40.820
All told, 101 Dalmatians has made $144 million
domestically, or just over $900 million when

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.120
adjusted for inflation.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.840
And we know… we're dropping a lot of numbers
on you but when it comes to this classic movie,

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.980
there's only one number that really matters.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.780
“Let's see now, that's 84, add 15 plus 2…
a hundred and one!”

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.780
“A hundred and one?"

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:03.379
The Force Awakens is the seventh film in the
extremely popular Star Wars franchise, and

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:07.629
the first sequel to the original series in
more than 30 years, bringing Harrison Ford,

00:03:07.629 --> 00:03:10.740
Carrie Fisher, and Mark Hamill back to the
roles that made them famous.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.680
Even for a Star Wars movie, expectations were
extremely high.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.760
"People are counting on us.

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:18.970
The galaxy is counting on us."

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:22.990
The Force Awakens was the first Star Wars
film to open during the holiday season and

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.860
it turned out to be a stroke of genius.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:29.180
While it had a record-breaking first weekend,
if you look closely at the numbers, the movie's

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.640
true accomplishment came in the months that
followed.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.560
After opening like a summer blockbuster, it
played like a holiday hit, continuing to earn

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.000
millions week after week.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.640
By the end of its explosive run, The Force
Awakens had earned $936 million domestically.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.900
Adjusted for inflation, that's $974 million
today.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.660
Disney has enjoyed a remarkable run of box
office success in the 21st century, but its

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.660
all-time biggest ticket-seller didn't come
from Marvel, Lucasfilm, or Pixar.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.160
No, their biggest hit comes from the golden
age of animation.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:06.100
In fact, it's their oldest movie — the animated
classic Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.340
Hollywood thought Walt Disney was nuts when
he set out to release a feature-length animated

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:10.840
film.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:15.420
Less than a decade after Mickey Mouse debuted
in "Steamboat Willie," cartoons were strictly

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.060
the domain of shorts that played before the
main feature.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.080
Disney changed all that — and the movie
business too — with Snow White.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.320
The animated classic earned impressive numbers
during its initial release in 1937, becoming

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.940
the top-grossing film that year.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:34.740
Snow White was re-released eight times, culminating
in a total domestic gross of more than $184

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:38.950
million, which would add up to $982 million
today.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.250
From an animated, family-friendly classic
to a Satanic horror film in which an innocent

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.240
young girl turns into a head-spinning, bile-spewing,
demonic little monster.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.200
Well, at least you can say American moviegoers
have eclectic tastes.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:57.020
One of the most influential horror films ever,
The Exorcist is still shocking and terrifying

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.240
today — but in 1973, its shock factor was
near scandalous.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.000
And all that scandal helped The Exorcist scare
up a ton of cash.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:10.440
Combining its initial run and a later re-release,
The Exorcist has now made more than $230 million

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:16.461
domestically, which rises up to $996 million
when adjusted for inflation, making it the

00:05:16.461 --> 00:05:19.480
all-time top ticket-seller among R-rated movies.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.420
The Exorcist isn't the highest-grossing, however.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.510
The R-rated film with that honor, ironically
enough, is The Passion of the Christ.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:32.400
Doctor Zhivago is the very definition of an
epic historical romance, a genre that doesn't

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.720
get a lot of love from movie studios anymore.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:35.500
Why's that?

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.100
Well, for one they're incredibly expensive.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.600
They're also very risky.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.900
Most problematic, you can't turn them into
a franchise.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.200
That said, when they hit, they hit big.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.820
Doctor Zhivago definitely qualifies.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.740
It features stunning visuals, captivating
melodrama, and beautiful leads playing lovers

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.000
whose doomed romance is torn asunder by the
winds of history.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.000
Based on Russian novelist Boris Pasternak's
book, and directed by the king of the epic,

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:05.910
David Lean, Doctor Zhivago stars Omar Sharif
and Julie Christie as people in love with

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.000
each other, but married to others, during
the Russian Revolution.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:14.760
It proved a significantly bigger hit than
Lean's other epics, earning $111 million — or

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.420
an adjusted gross of over $1 billion.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.900
Incredibly, it wasn't even the biggest hit
of 1965… but more on that later.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.140
For now, it's safe to say that Doctor Zhivago
was the Titanic of its time, full of romance

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.380
and tragedy… just without any unfortunate
catch phrases.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.540
"I'm king of the world!"

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:39.420
When Jaws popped up in 1975, it practically
invented the summer movie season, breaking

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:43.901
box office records — and frightening people
away from beaches, lakes, ponds, and even

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:44.820
a few pools.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:49.640
Based on Peter Benchley's novel of the same
name, the problem-plagued production nearly

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:53.830
ended relative newcomer Steven Spielberg's
career, but ultimately, all the technical

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.550
snafus forced the young director to get inventive.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:01.080
By creating a theater of the mind, he made
movie history, delivering suspenseful moments

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.930
that were far more effective because he was
forced to keep his toothy antagonist offscreen

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:05.780
for much of the film.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.960
Spielberg's improvisation worked: Jaws opened
on June 20, 1975 and quickly blew up to an

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.280
inflation-adjusted gross of $1.153 billion.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.420
By any measure, calling that a boatload of
cash would be an understatement.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.600
"You're going to need a bigger boat."

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.340
Director Cecil B. DeMille's name is practically
synonymous with "epic," as his lavish productions

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:33.440
achieved a scope and grandeur that will simply
never be matched by the CGI pumping up today's

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:33.940
blockbusters.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.080
He was also a risk-taker, and The Ten Commandments
is proof.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:41.590
Who else but DeMille would remake one of the
biggest movies of all time, especially when

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.980
he was the one who directed the original version
in 1923?

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:50.300
More than thirty years later, the film industry
was facing stiff competition from TV and movies

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.900
had to get bigger to compete.

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:55.760
And thanks to DeMille, The Ten Commandments
is as big as it gets.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:01.880
It opened on October 5, 1956, and earned $85
million by the end of its run, easily becoming

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:05.550
the highest-grossing film of the year and
one of the most enduring biblical portraits

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:06.780
in the history of cinema.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.520
When you think of Moses on film, you think
of DeMille's The Ten Commandments.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.360
Well… you might also think of this...

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:22.980
"Lord Jehovah has given unto you, these fifteen…
oy… these ten, ten commandments!”

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.640
If you were alive in the late 1990s, you'll
always remember Titanic.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.390
Costing a record-breaking $200 million to
produce, the film was viewed by many as a

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:37.070
career-sinking folly for director James Cameron
— and those predictions seemed to be confirmed

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.040
when it opened to an unimpressive $28 million
on December 19th, 1997.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:47.180
But then something strange happened: Titanic
didn't sink… well, the movie didn't.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:52.280
The next weekend, the film made 24 percent
more than the weekend before — and it just

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:57.130
kept on selling tickets, as millions of people
would tackle the 194-minute runtime to see

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:58.360
it again and again.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:03.820
By the time it was all said and done, Titanic
made what was then an unfathomable $600 million

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:04.800
domestically.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:10.940
The film was later re-released in 3D and a
20th anniversary reissue followed in 2017.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:17.070
While Titanic's box office ship has sailed,
its total haul now stands at $659 million,

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.080
or $1.221 billion when adjusted for inflation.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.740
Film buffs will argue over Steven Spielberg's
defining masterpiece for decades to come.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:29.390
What isn't up for debate is his biggest hit
— E.T.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:30.860
The Extra-Terrestrial.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.780
In a career full of box office glory, including
one record-breaking mega-hit after another,

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:39.320
the fact that E.T. is Spielberg's biggest
blockbuster says a lot.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:45.640
It opened to $11 million on June 11th, 1982,
and much like Titanic, kept making money.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:50.760
It holds the record for most non-consecutive
weekends at No. 1 and fluctuated between the

00:09:50.771 --> 00:09:54.760
first and second positions on the box office
charts for five solid months.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:10:00.340
By the end of its first theatrical run, E.T.
had earned $359 million, passing Star Wars

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:01.900
as the highest-grossing film ever.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:09.840
E.T. earned another $70 million in re-releases,
bringing its total to $435 million, or $1.278

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.320
billion adjusted for inflation—which is
something you'd definitely want to phone home

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:14.820
about.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:16.100
"E.T. phone home."

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.320
"E.T. phone home."

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:18.740
"E.T. phone home."

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.680
The 1960s are remembered as an era of rebellion,
experimentation, and free love.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.230
But when it came to movies, the biggest hits
were the ones you could watch with your parents

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:31.100
and that was never more true than in 1965.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:36.200
Doctor Zhivago was enormously successful with
its mix of history and romance...but it was

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:40.520
beat out by The Sound of Music, the most successful
movie musical of all time.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:46.280
The film is based on Rodgers and Hammerstein's
classic 1959 play, and while it took six years

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:50.360
to go from Broadway to the big screen, the
movie's producers were not disappointed.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:56.460
The Sound of Music was a blockbuster, opening
wide on March 2nd, 1965 and ultimately earning

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:03.470
approximately $159 million through all of
its releases for a total of $1.283 billion

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:04.460
in today's dough-re-mi.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:09.200
Star Wars is arguably the most popular franchise
ever.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.020
It's second only to the Marvel Cinematic Universe
in total box office numbers.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:18.760
As mentioned previously, the phenomenon began
in 1977 with Star Wars: A New Hope.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.310
Initially, only a handful of theaters ordered
the movie.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:26.290
In fact, it opened in only 43 venues on the
same weekend as a surefire summer hit, Smokey

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:27.020
and the Bandit.

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:32.160
Fox was convinced that their sci-fi swashbuckler
would be crushed by the competition.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.540
History had other ideas.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.040
Star Wars earned $1.5 million in its opening
weekend.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:41.240
Fox broadened its release to meet demand,
which kept growing.... and growing.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:48.120
During its first 18-month run, the film stayed
on target to make a mind-boggling $307 million,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.420
which grew with five re-releases over the
course of 20 years.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:57.760
All told, Star Wars earned $460 million, and
after we adjust that for inflation, it equals

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.000
$1.604 billion dollars.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.340
"If money is all that you love, then that
is what you'll receive."

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:07.500
There can be only one winner, and it's hard
to fathom that any movie will ever sell more

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.220
tickets than Gone with the Wind.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:13.480
Based on Margaret Mitchell's bestselling novel
of the same name, with a pretty substantial

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:18.720
budget of $3.9 million, and starring the King
of Hollywood himself, Clark Gable, everybody

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.760
knew Gone with the Wind was going to be a
hit… it was just a matter of how big.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:29.400
MGM got its answer when 300,000 people arrived
for the premiere in Atlanta on December 15,

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:29.900
1939.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:35.260
Its initial run earned mammoth numbers, inspiring
multiple re-releases through several decades.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.200
All told, the movie has earned $200 million.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:43.300
That's more than most releases today, but
it's truly mind-boggling considering its adjusted

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:49.420
gross would be $1.822 billion… which should
be extremely impressive to anyone.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:51.840
“Frankly my dear, I don't give a damn."

